          "Drag session fails to end",
          "I don't yet have STR, but it's happened to me twice in the last 2 days. After using my browser for some period of time, the browser ends up stuck in a d&d session which is never ended, and attempts to drag things stop working.  Opening the browser console and running `Cc[\\\\\"@mozilla.org/widget/dragservice;1\\\\\"].getService(Ci.nsIDragService).getCurrentSession().endDragSession(true)` causes the browser to start accepting new drags again.\\\\n\\\\nI don't know exactly what causes this to occur, but I figured I'd file a bug anyway.\\\\n\\\\nI'm running Fedora using X11, and have Fission enabled.",
          "P2"
        ],
        [
          "1732766",
          "2021-09-27 18:30:12+00:00",
          "Make worker private to be aware of the allowListed storage access permission",
          "In Bug 1731557, we have introduced the storage permission states to reflect the fact where did the storage permission come from, storage access permission, or allowListed.  Currently, the worker private only knows if it has the storage access, but doesn't know where did it come from. So, we should make the worker private to know the storage permission state.",
          "P3"
        ],
        [
          "1732785",
          "2021-09-27 20:30:39+00:00",
          "Consider clobbering registers before jump to catch handler in throw stub",
          "In the wasm throw stub we will perform a longjmp to the nearest enclosing wasm catch handler. As a security hardening measure, we should consider clobbering registers immediately before the jump [1] to mitigate possible regalloc bugs in the function we're jumping to. There could be other security issues this would help out with as well, the regalloc one just seems the most likely.\\\\n\\\\nTwo questions if we do this:\\\\n  1. Which registers? I think we could clobber all GPR's. I don't think we need to do floating point registers.\\\\n  2. What value to clobber with? I think zero is the safest, but maybe there's a sentinel value that could help us identify crashes with?\\\\n\\\\nI don't think this would be a code bloat issue as this is once per-module. I don't think we would be concerned with performance either, as we're intentionally not concerned with performance of the exceptional path.\\\\n\\\\n[1] https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/rev/1df999af9999ccb436512cfece57a68d94d36e08/js/src/wasm/WasmStubs.cpp#2857",
          "P3"
        ]
      ]
